2.text:
The two inbred parental strains contribute pure haplotypes that we denote as \(00000\) and \(11111\). The F1 animals are therefore uniformly heterozygous at all five SNPs, carrying exactly these two haplotypes.

Every gamete is produced by a meiosis that places exactly one crossover between the two homologs. With five ordered SNPs there are four possible intervals in which that crossover can occur. Consequently the eight possible F1 gametes are the single-switch strings
\[
\begin{align*}
&01111,00111,00011,00001,\\
&10000,11000,11100,11110.
\end{align*}
\]
(The two pure parental strings are excluded because a crossover is obligatory.)

An F2 individual therefore carries any unordered pair drawn from the eight strings above. When that F2 individual produces gametes, a second obligatory crossover occurs between whatever pair of haplotypes it happens to possess. The resulting gametic haplotypes can therefore contain at most two switches. All binary strings of length 5 fall into three exhaustive classes according to the number of switches they contain:

- 0 switches: \(00000,11111\) (2 strings);
- 1 switch: 8 strings (the same set already enumerated above);
- 2 switches: 12 strings (the six compositions of 5 into three positive integers, each realized in both starting polarities).

Hence exactly \(2+8+12=22\) distinct sequences are possible. Because the two successive meioses can be arranged (by appropriate choice of mates) to realize every combination of switch locations, every one of these 22 strings actually appears among the chromosomes of F3 animals. The restriction to a single recombination orientation merely fixes the reference strand; it does not alter the set of attainable allele patterns on that strand.

Thus the number of unique sequences present in the F3 generation is 22.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

