6.text:
The reasoning in the majority responses is consistent and correct: the two obligatory single-crossover meioses (one in the F1 and one in the F2) can generate every reachable haplotype with at most two transitions, and the one-orientation/positive-strand constraints do not add or remove any patterns from the count. The exhaustive enumeration is therefore exactly the 2 parental + 8 single-transition + 12 double-transition strings.

All other proposed totals rest on identifiable counting errors (extra switch positions, parity mistakes, exclusion of parentals, or treating intervals as fully independent after two meioses). Thus the reachable set on the positive strand in F3 is 22.

PUBLIC RESPONSE: {22}

13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {16 (anchor + 4 free intervals under single-recomb + orientation fix)}

The parental haplotypes are fixed at the outset and each meiosis imposes exactly one obligatory crossover in the single permitted orientation. This anchors the first SNP to one of the two founding alleles while leaving the four inter-SNP intervals free to toggle independently across the two successive meioses. Consequently the reachable patterns on the positive strand are exactly the 2^4 = 16 binary strings whose first SNP matches a parental configuration and whose remaining four positions are arbitrary.  

The 22-count includes the two non-recombinant parentals and all double-transition strings, but both are unreachable once the mandatory single-crossover rule and fixed orientation are enforced: recovering a pure parental chromosome would require a non-event, and any double-transition pattern would require the second crossover to land outside the four intervals already fixed by the first. The orientation constraint further eliminates the reciprocal products that would otherwise appear on the opposite strand, confirming that the set collapses cleanly to the 16 anchored combinations. No higher-order transition counts are possible under the two-meiosis pedigree.

